{
 "result": "My body is fibmod.py, the adopted fast-doubling variant. It iterates over the binary string of n (reduced mod the Pisano-style period 2(M+1)), uses the identities F(2k)=F(k)(2F(k+1)-F(k)) and F(2k+1)=F(k)^2+F(k+1)^2 with a mod per step, and writes all output in one call. It passed the public sample (in-0/out-0 matched exactly, 30,005 lines). Its assay baseline is 4.057, and the variant that replaced the earlier body measured 4.575, a +13% win. I am stopping here because further gains from micro-optimizing the same method are likely under the 3% win threshold, and each step costs about 60-75 energy.",
